--TEST--
`??+=`, `??-=` and `??.=` refuse what their operator refuses, with the same code: a string under `+`, a value whose result does not fit the target, mixed signedness, an enum, a `bool`, a `secret` value appended into a plain array element, a new element `$a[]`, a write through `?->`, and a `??` written as the target —`rule:expressions/defaulting-assignment`, `rule:types/arithmetic`, `rule:security/secret-propagation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
enum Size: int { Small = 1, Large = 2 }

class Post {
    public ?int $views = null;
}

class Shop {
    public function m(secret string $key, ?Post $post): void {
        // A string has no `+`, and "" is a string.
        ?string $name = null;
        $name ??+= 1;
        // The result of `.` is a string, and a string does not fit `?int`.
        ?int $count = null;
        $count ??.= "x";
        // `uint` and `int` have no common type.
        ?uint $u = null;
        int $i = 1;
        $u ??+= $i;
        // An enum case is not a number.
        ?Size $size = null;
        $size ??+= 1;
        // A `bool` has no `-`.
        ?bool $flag = null;
        $flag ??-= 1;
        // A `secret` value may not go into a plain array element.
        array<string> $notes = [];
        $notes["k"] ??.= $key;
        // `$list[]` is a new element, and a new element can only be assigned to.
        array<int> $list = [];
        $list[] ??+= 1;
        // A write through `?->` is not allowed.
        $post?->views ??+= 1;
        // A `??` is not a place to write to, so no syntax sets another default.
        ?int $a = null;
        ($a ?? 100) -= 1;
    }
}
